# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# You probably need nothing here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `next dev` already loads .env.development, which points the app at the
# Firebase emulators (demo-degen-inc) — run `npm run emulators` alongside it.
# On App Hosting, the backend supplies what the app needs by itself: see
# apphosting.yaml.
#
# Copy this to .env.local only to override something locally. .env.local wins
# over .env.development and is never committed.

# The browser SDK's web-app config, as JSON — from the Firebase console, under
# Project settings -> Your apps. Public by design: it names the project, and
# what anyone may do with it is decided by Authentication and the rules. It is
# inlined at build time. Setting it here (with NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_EMULATORS
# unset) points `next dev` at a real project instead of the emulators.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_CONFIG='{"apiKey":"...","authDomain":"...","projectId":"...","storageBucket":"...","appId":"..."}'

# Point the browser SDK at the emulators.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_EMULATORS=true


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRETS do NOT belong in this file, or in any committed file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-only values are read at request time, never inlined, and must never
# carry a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix — that would ship them to every visitor. In
# production they live in Cloud Secret Manager:
#
#   firebase apphosting:secrets:set STRIPE_SECRET_KEY
#
# and apphosting.yaml references them by name. None exist yet; Stripe's two
# keys will be the first.
